    "q-126768": {
      "dom_id": "question-126768",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Using the first two non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $f(x),$ approximate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Write your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\,≈\\,\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We have that\n\n$$\nf(x)≈{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ by integrating the above series term-by-term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ≈{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{42}{x}^{6})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{42}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{42}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
