    "q-126769": {
      "dom_id": "question-126769",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that the Maclaurin expansion of $f(x)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}{(2n+1)!},\n$$\n\ncalculate the radius of convergence $R$ of this power series.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $R\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To find the radius of convergence, we apply the ratio test.\n\nSo, let's denote ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}{(2n+1)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{4n+6}}{(2n+3)!}\\cdot \\frac{(2n+1)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(2n+2)(2n+3)}| \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{1}{(2n+2)(2n+3)}| \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
